-- title:
--   Theorem 6.1 — if every π ≠ 0 has some π·v_k < 0, the iterates are bounded (stated for (2.7); the paper prints (2.8))
-- statement:
--   Let $w(\pi)=\min_k\{c_k+\pi\cdot v_k\}$ on $E^n$ be bounded above, and suppose that for every $\pi\ne0$ there is an index $k$ with $\pi\cdot v_k<0$. Then every sequence $\{\pi^j\}$ generated by the subgradient algorithm (2.6), $\pi^{j+1}=\pi^j+t_j v_{k(j)}$ with $k(j)$ attaining the minimum in (2.2) at $\pi^j$, with positive step sizes satisfying (2.7) ($t_j\to0$, $\sum_j t_j=\infty$), is bounded:
--   $$\sup_j |\pi^j|<\infty.$$
--
--   Boundedness of the iterates is the hypothesis of Lemma 6.2 and Theorem 6.3; this result supplies a checkable condition on the data $v_k$ alone that guarantees it (the paper checks it for the traveling-salesman formulation).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The paper prints the theorem for sequences satisfying "(2.6), (2.8)", where (2.8) is the step rule $t_j=\lambda_j(\hat w-w(\pi^j))/|v(\pi^j)|^2$. Its proof, however, uses $w(\pi^j)\to w^*$, which is the consequence of (2.7) stated just before the theorem and the standing assumption of Section 6; under (2.8) with $\hat w<w^*$ this limit does not hold in general. The statement here is therefore made for step sizes satisfying (2.7). "$w$ bounded above" is the paper-wide standing assumption (p. 64).
-- source:
--   Held, Wolfe & Crowder, Validation of subgradient optimization, Math. Programming 6 (1974), p. 80, Theorem 6.1 (hypothesis (2.8) replaced by the §6 standing assumption (2.7), see the Formalization Note)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HeldWolfeCrowder_CoreProblem_Setting

namespace HeldWolfeCrowder.CoreProblem

open scoped InnerProductSpace
open Filter Topology

/-- **Held, Wolfe & Crowder (1974), Theorem 6.1, p. 80 — stated for the step-size rule (2.7).**
The paper prints the hypothesis "(2.6), (2.8)", but its proof uses `w(π^j) → w*`, which is the
consequence of (2.7) quoted just above the theorem (§6's standing assumption); this statement
uses (2.7). If for every `π ≠ 0` some `v_k` has `π·v_k < 0`, and `w` is bounded above, then
every sequence `{π^j}` generated by (2.6) with positive step sizes satisfying (2.7) is bounded. -/
theorem bounded_iterates {n : ℕ} {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [Nonempty ι]
    (c : ι → ℝ) (v : ι → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (hw : BddAbove (Set.range (w c v)))
    (hdir : ∀ π : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n), π ≠ 0 → ∃ k, ⟪π, v k⟫_ℝ < 0)
    (t : ℕ → ℝ) (π : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (k : ℕ → ι)
    (hrun : IsSubgradientRun c v t π k) (ht : StepSizeCond t) :
    Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range π) := by sorry

end HeldWolfeCrowder.CoreProblem
